INTERVIEW COPILOT — MASTER PRODUCT ROADMAP

Execution plan for AI coding agents • Phase-by-phase • Scope-locked

1. Product Direction

Build a specialist real-time interview copilot that combines a simple pre-interview setup, a minimal live interview HUD, robust speech/question understanding, strict turn isolation, domain-specific practitioner knowledge, and candidate-fact safety.

Primary product principle: setup complexity belongs before the interview; live complexity must disappear during the interview.

2. Target User Flow

Dashboard → Create Session → Pre-flight → Live Interview HUD → Question Understanding → Answer Engine → Turn/Context Management → Post-interview Review

3. Engine Pipeline

Audio → STT → Question Boundary Detection → Semantic Question Reconstruction → Turn Commit → Context Resolution → Knowledge Retrieval → Answer Generation → Fact Safety → Live HUD

4. Non-Negotiable Existing Invariants

CommittedInterviewTurn remains immutable after COMMIT_FINAL.

Every committed turn has a unique turnId.

Every answer request belongs to exactly one turnId/requestId.

Late responses cannot overwrite another active turn.

Manual Answer must resolve the immutable committed-turn snapshot.

Future transcript must never leak into an earlier answer request.

Follow-up inheritance must be explicit; unrelated topic switches clear scenario context.

5. Execution Roadmap

PHASE 0 — Freeze Baseline & Regression Gate

Goal: protect the working engine before adding product features.

Scope:

Capture current test baseline and diagnostics.

Lock turn isolation, manual answer safety, follow-up inheritance and response ownership.

Create a mandatory regression command/checklist for every later phase.

DO NOT TOUCH:

STT behavior

Answer prompting

Retrieval logic

Turn isolation architecture unless a failing regression proves it is necessary

Acceptance:

Existing test/typecheck/lint/build gates remain green.

A documented baseline exists.

Every later phase must run this regression gate before completion.

PHASE 1 — Create Session

Goal: introduce a clean pre-interview configuration workflow.

Session setup:

Session Type: Interview / Regular Call

Company

Job Description

Resume

Knowledge Documents / Practitioner Playbooks

Custom Instructions

Question Type: Technical / Behavioral / Situational

Answer Format: Full Script / Script + Bullets / Bullets

Length: Short / Balanced / Long

Tone: Simple / Formal

STAR Method toggle where relevant

Model

Language

Auto Answer

Save Transcript

Architecture:

Create a SessionConfig model.

Session starts from an immutable/snapshotted configuration.

Resume/Documents/Instructions are scoped to the session.

Allow reopen and duplicate session.

DO NOT TOUCH:

STT

QuestionCommitGate

Context classifier

Answer reasoning

Turn isolation

Acceptance:

Create, save, reopen and duplicate a session.

Session settings persist after app restart.

Starting a session uses exactly the selected configuration.

No engine regressions.

PHASE 2 — Live Interview HUD

Goal: create a low-cognitive-load floating interface for the real interview.

Default HUD:

Listening / paused state

Session timer

Current interpreted question

Suggested answer

Answer

Regenerate

Shorter

Clear

Previous / Next turn

Window controls:

Pin

Resize

Opacity

Hide

Compact mode

Keyboard shortcuts

Optional click-through mode

Optional later actions:

Screenshot

Chat

Rules:

Do not expose developer diagnostics by default.

Do not place complex session settings in the HUD.

Learn from successful interview-overlay interaction patterns, but do not copy another product's branding, exact layout, icons, colors, typography or pixel design.

Acceptance:

Usable at small overlay size.

Keyboard-first operation works.

Manual Answer remains bound to the correct turnId.

Late responses remain attached to their original turn.

No regression in current engine.

PHASE 3 — Semantic Question Reconstruction

Goal: recover interviewer intent when STT is imperfect.

Pipeline:

RAW TRANSCRIPT → RECONSTRUCTION → INTERPRETED QUESTION → CONFIDENCE → COMMIT

Example:

Raw: “Tại sao em không chọn Romania?”

Prior context: Domain A vs Domain B

Interpreted: “Tại sao em không chọn Domain A?”

Confidence: 0.96

Policy:

High confidence: allow semantic correction.

Medium confidence: preserve raw text and candidate interpretation.

Low confidence: do not silently rewrite.

Reconstruction must use bounded context and known entities.

Never let the reconstruction model invent a new scenario.

Developer telemetry:

rawTranscript

interpretedQuestion

reconstructionConfidence

evidence/linked entities

turnId

Acceptance:

Robust entity recovery in adversarial STT cases.

No silent high-impact correction at low confidence.

Regression tests for domain/entity/numeric corrections.

No contamination across turns.

PHASE 4 — Interview Context Graph

Goal: move from simple previous-turn memory to explicit scenario-aware interview memory.

Models:

scenarioId

turnId

parentTurnId

referencedTurnIds

scenario facts/entities

explicit topic-switch boundary

Behavior:

“Tại sao?” inherits only the required parent scenario.

“Còn backlink?” resolves the correct prior subject.

A new budget/PBN/GSC scenario does not inherit expired-domain facts.

Interviewer references such as “con A”, “case lúc nãy”, “mốc ngày 10” resolve to explicit prior claims where confidence is sufficient.

Acceptance:

Multi-turn scenario tests.

Topic-switch isolation.

Correct parent resolution for short follow-ups.

Zero unrelated scenario-fact leakage.

PHASE 5 — Answer Grounding & Fact Safety

Goal: make answers practical without turning risk signals into invented facts or invented candidate experience.

Fact provenance:

INTERVIEWER_FACT

RESUME_FACT

PRACTITIONER_DOCUMENT

GENERAL_KNOWLEDGE

INFERENCE

UNSUPPORTED_CANDIDATE_CLAIM

Rules:

Risk signal ≠ proven penalty.

Practitioner experience ≠ candidate personal experience.

Never invent metrics, budgets, rankings, dates, projects or achievements.

If evidence is insufficient, state the next verification rather than inventing the missing fact.

Answer actions:

Shorter

More Technical

Explain Why

Give Example

Defend Answer

Regenerate

All actions regenerate the same immutable turn snapshot.

Acceptance:

Candidate Fact Firewall tests.

Unsupported-claim tests.

Contradiction tests.

Answer action tests preserve turn ownership.

PHASE 6 — Advanced Live Interview UX

Goal: improve real-world usability after the core is stable.

Candidate features:

Speaker separation

Interviewer challenge detection

Interruption handling

Natural-pause confidence

Answer-ready indicator

Near-camera positioning

Multi-monitor behavior

Compact / discreet mode

Better shortcut customization

Challenge detection example:

“PBN là gì?” → knowledge explanation

“Anh không đồng ý, site chưa có traffic mà em đi PBN để làm gì?” → defend/reason response

Acceptance:

Features are individually gated.

No feature may weaken turn isolation or question-boundary correctness.

PHASE 7 — Post-Interview Intelligence

Goal: turn each interview into feedback for the next one.

Review:

Transcript

Questions and answers

Weak answers

Regenerated answers

Misheard/reconstructed questions

Unsupported claims

Interviewer challenges

Knowledge gaps

Suggested practitioner-playbook improvements

Latency summary

Feedback loop:

Interview → detect weakness → review → improve playbook/config → next interview

Acceptance:

Review data is derived from recorded session events.

Never fabricate interview events or missing transcript.

6. Developer Mode

Developer Mode is separate from the production interview HUD.

Raw transcript

Interpreted question

STT / reconstruction confidence

turnId / parentTurnId / scenarioId

Intent

Retrieved sources and provenance

RequestId

STT / reconstruction / retrieval / LLM / total latency

Commit / hold / cancellation / render telemetry

7. Mandatory AI-Agent Task Template

Every coding-agent task must contain these sections. Do not give the agent broad prompts such as “make it like Parakeet and improve context.”

GOAL
One concrete outcome only.

SCOPE
Exact files/components/behaviors allowed to change.

DO NOT TOUCH
Explicit protected subsystems.

IMPLEMENTATION CONSTRAINTS
Existing invariants that must remain true.

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
Observable behavior required for completion.

REGRESSION GATE
Tests, typecheck, lint, build and diagnostics that must pass.

REPORT
Root cause/implementation summary, changed files, tests added, before/after evidence, git status.

IMPORTANT
First audit the existing implementation and report a minimal plan.
Do not begin broad refactoring.
Do not add unrelated features.
Do not declare COMPLETE until acceptance criteria and regression gates pass.

8. Recommended Execution Order

Execute strictly in this order: Phase 0 → Phase 1 → Phase 2 → live regression test → Phase 3 → Phase 4 → Phase 5 → Phase 6 → Phase 7.

Do not combine Phase 1 and Phase 3, or Phase 2 and Phase 5, in a single coding task. Finish, verify and commit each phase before starting the next.

9. Immediate Next Action

Give this document to the coding agent and ask it to read the entire roadmap. Then instruct it to execute PHASE 0 only. After the Phase 0 report is reviewed and accepted, issue a separate instruction for PHASE 1. Repeat phase by phase.

10. Product North Star

Hear the right question → understand the right context → generate a grounded answer → show it with minimal cognitive load during the live interview.